 Pulse
Predictive maintenance that turns early warnings into safe, actionable maintenance
The idea in 30 seconds
Pulse monitors industrial equipment, learns how each machine actually operates, and detects early degradation before failure.
Unlike systems that stop at an anomaly alert, Pulse explains the evidence, asks an experienced technician to validate it, checks whether the plant is ready for maintenance, and prepares a review-ready work order with the required parts, people, isolation, permits, and maintenance window.
Pulse detects the problem early and helps the team act on it safely.
The problem
Manufacturing plants collect large amounts of temperature, pressure, vibration, flow, and equipment-status data. Predictive-maintenance systems can identify abnormal behaviour, but their alerts often fail to become timely maintenance action.
There are four reasons.
1. Equipment does not always operate at manual values
A manual may list normal pressure as 7 bar, while a particular machine has safely operated around 9 bar for months because of the actual process configuration.
A static threshold can therefore:
Raise repeated false alarms at 9 bar
Miss a meaningful drop from 9 bar to 7 bar
Miss slow degradation that remains below the design limit
Pulse learns the normal operating behaviour of each asset while continuing to respect approved safety limits.
2. An alert does not explain what is happening
“Pump P-101A: high vibration” is not enough.
The technician needs to know:
What changed?
How quickly is it changing?
Which signals support the alert?
Is it likely to be equipment degradation, a process change, or a faulty sensor?
Has a similar pattern appeared before?
How long can the equipment continue operating?
3. Field expertise is missing from the model
Experienced operators often recognize a machine problem from sound, smell, vibration, process behaviour, or knowledge of a recent operating change.
Pulse should not overrule that experience. It should combine model evidence with technician observations and let the technician confirm, correct, or reject the diagnosis.
4. Prediction does not automatically produce action
Even a correct prediction still requires:
A clear maintenance scope
The correct spare part and tools
A qualified technician
A production window
Isolation and stored-energy checks
Permit requirements
Awareness of other work in the area
Testing and return-to-service steps
Without this planning, an alert becomes another item in the backlog.
Proposed solution
Pulse is a human-in-the-loop predictive-maintenance copilot.
Its workflow is:
Detect → Explain → Validate → Plan → Act → Learn
1. Detect degradation against the real operating baseline
Pulse consumes live or simulated sensor data such as:
Vibration
Temperature
Pressure
Flow rate
Motor current
Equipment state
It learns the normal range and behaviour of each asset under different operating conditions.
Pulse looks for:
Sudden deviations
Slow drift
Unusual combinations of signals
Change in the relationship between variables
Behaviour that differs from similar operating cycles
It distinguishes three boundaries:
Boundary
Meaning
Design limit
Approved operating or safety limit from engineering documents
Learned baseline
How this specific asset normally behaves in the field
Current condition
What the asset is doing now

This lets Pulse detect behavioural change before the equipment reaches a critical limit.
2. Explain the alert with evidence
Pulse produces a concise explanation instead of a generic alarm:
“Drive-end vibration on P-101A has increased by 31% over five days. Motor current and bearing temperature are also rising under comparable load. The pattern differs from this pump’s six-month operating baseline and resembles two previous bearing-wear cases. The pressure remains within the approved safety limit.”
The explanation includes:
Signals that changed
Time and rate of change
Comparison with the asset’s baseline
Relevant design or safety limits
Similar maintenance history
Possible failure modes
Confidence and uncertainty
Missing evidence
RAG grounds document-based information in manuals, SOPs, and previous maintenance records. Sensor conclusions come from structured time-series analysis, not from the language model guessing numerical trends.
3. Ask the technician to validate the diagnosis
The technician can respond using text, voice, or a simple form:
Confirm the suspected issue
Correct the failure mode
Add an observation such as unusual noise or leakage
Explain a recent process change
Mark the alert as a sensor problem
Request an inspection before repair
Example:
“The sound is coming from the drive-end bearing. This started after the last product change. Inspect alignment before replacing the bearing.”
Pulse adds this observation to the case and preserves who provided it. The technician’s feedback also improves future alerts and maintenance history.
4. Assess the impact of the intervention
Once the issue is validated, Pulse checks whether the proposed maintenance action is practical and safe.
It considers:
Standby-equipment availability
Upstream and downstream dependencies
Shared isolation points
Active permits and nearby work
Degraded safeguards
Parts and tools
Technician skills and availability
Production schedule
Time required for isolation, repair, testing, and reinstatement
This is a lightweight dependency check, not a full plant simulation.
Pulse can compare options:
Option
Benefit
Constraint
Stop immediately
Lowest equipment-failure exposure
Standby pump unavailable
Reduce load and repair at 18:00
Preserves production until standby returns
Requires enhanced monitoring
Repair tomorrow
Best staffing availability
Longer degradation exposure

The maintenance planner or supervisor selects the option.
5. Prepare a review-ready work order
Pulse drafts:
Asset and failure description
Recommended maintenance task
Evidence and technician observations
Priority and target completion time
Required skills and estimated effort
Parts, tools, and materials
Suggested maintenance window
Candidate isolation and stored-energy checks
Suggested permit and PPE requirements
Conflicts with other work
Inspection and acceptance criteria
Testing and return-to-service steps
Required approvals
Pulse prepares the work. Authorized people review and approve it.
6. Learn from the outcome
When the job is completed, the technician records:
What was actually found
What action was performed
Parts consumed
Whether the original prediction was correct
Whether the asset returned to normal behaviour
Pulse uses this outcome to improve the asset baseline, alert confidence, similar-case retrieval, and future maintenance recommendations.
Safety boundary
Pulse assists with detection, explanation, planning, and documentation. It does not take safety-critical control.
A technician validates the diagnosis.
An authorized specialist reviews isolation requirements.
Authorized personnel physically apply locks and tags.
A competent person verifies isolation and safe conditions.
The responsible authority approves the permit.
Pulse does not operate valves, breakers, or process controls.
The hackathon demo
Scenario
Pump P-101A normally operates around 9 bar, although an old reference document lists 7 bar as a nominal value.
Over five simulated days:
Drive-end vibration rises gradually.
Bearing temperature increases.
Motor current becomes less stable.
Pressure remains around the usual 9 bar.
Demo flow
Sensor data streams into Pulse.
Pulse learns that 9 bar is normal for this asset, so it does not raise a false pressure alarm.
Pulse detects the combined vibration, temperature, and current trend.
It shows the evidence and suggests bearing degradation or misalignment.
The technician adds: “The drive-end bearing sounds rough; check alignment first.”
Pulse checks the proposed maintenance action.
It finds that the standby pump is unavailable until 17:00 and nearby hot work ends at 16:30.
Pulse compares immediate shutdown with reduced-load operation until an 18:00 window.
The planner selects 18:00.
Pulse generates a review-ready work order with parts, skills, checks, and approvals.
The technician closes the job with the actual finding, completing the learning loop.
The wow factor
The demonstration contains three memorable moments:
It understands actual field behaviour
Pulse knows that 9 bar is normal for this pump even though a document says 7 bar.
It combines AI with technician expertise
The system finds the trend, but the technician contributes the field diagnosis and can correct the AI.
It goes from prediction to safe action
Pulse does not stop at “the pump may fail.” It identifies a practical intervention window and prepares the maintenance work.
Predictive maintenance tells the team that a machine may fail. Pulse helps them understand it, validate it, and act before it fails.
Why this is stronger than a standard predictive-maintenance project
Standard project
Pulse
