MVP scope
Build one complete scenario with:
Two duty/standby pumps
Five sensor streams
One injected degradation pattern
One operating-baseline mismatch
One manual and two procedures
Three historical maintenance cases
Two technician observations
One parts inventory
Two active work or permit records
One generated work order
Depth is more important than the number of assets.
Three-day execution plan
Day 1 — Data and detection
Finalize the pump-degradation scenario and generate normal and failing sensor data.
Define the assets, operating baseline, parts, technicians, and maintenance history.
Stream sensor events through Pub/Sub and store them in BigQuery.
Implement baseline comparison and anomaly scoring.
Day 2 — AI explanation and maintenance planning
Load the manual, procedures, and historical maintenance cases into the retrieval layer.
Generate evidence-grounded alert explanations.
Add technician confirmation, correction, and field observations.
Implement checks for standby equipment, parts, schedules, permits, and work conflicts.
Generate intervention options and the draft work order.
Day 3 — Integration and demo
Build the dashboard for asset health, evidence, technician validation, options, and work order.
Run and test the complete detect-to-action flow.
Test a false alarm and a missing-data case.
Polish the three-minute demonstration and architecture diagram.
Record a backup video and complete the submission.
Suggested two-person split
Person 1 — Data, detection, and backend
Sensor-data generator
Pub/Sub and BigQuery
Baseline and anomaly model
Planning rules and Cloud Run APIs
Person 2 — AI and product experience
RAG and alert explanations
Technician-feedback flow
Work-order generation
Dashboard and demo story
What we will not build
A full industrial simulation platform
Automatic permit approval
Automatic physical isolation
Real control-system integration
A generic chatbot over manuals
Several shallow equipment scenarios
A complex model requiring unavailable real-world failure data
Research basis
Hazardous-energy control requires documented procedures, authorized personnel, and verification before maintenance begins: OSHA 1910.147
Isolation planning must account for job scope, energy sources, isolation points, residual energy, responsibility, and changing conditions: Isolation Management in the Permit to Work Process
Human-in-the-loop industrial AI should combine operator judgment with machine assistance: Human-in-the-loop and large language models in smart manufacturing
Effective predictive maintenance depends on field observations, work history, planning, scheduling, and execution readiness: Predictive Maintenance Implementation

